1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,690
This is the Angel of Independence,
in the heart of Mexico City.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:09,050
When it was finished in nineteen ten,
you climbed nine steps to reach its base.

3
00:00:09,926 --> 00:00:11,696
Today, there are fourteen more.

4
00:00:11,826 --> 00:00:13,516
Nobody made the monument taller.

5
00:00:13,626 --> 00:00:16,156
Its foundations go deep,
down to firmer ground,

6
00:00:16,206 --> 00:00:17,896
so the column stayed where it was.

7
00:00:18,206 --> 00:00:19,456
The city around it sank.

8
00:00:20,363 --> 00:00:24,093
Mexico City is one of the
fastest-sinking big cities on Earth.

9
00:00:24,383 --> 00:00:28,713
In some neighborhoods, the ground drops
by up to half a meter every single year.

10
00:00:29,527 --> 00:00:31,877
Venice sinks by a few millimeters a year.

11
00:00:32,067 --> 00:00:35,157
This city, in places, more
than a hundred times faster.

12
00:00:35,367 --> 00:00:38,977
So why is a city of more than twenty
million people falling into the ground?

13
00:00:42,483 --> 00:00:44,403
The answer starts seven hundred years ago,

14
00:00:44,443 --> 00:00:45,173
with water.

15
00:00:45,719 --> 00:00:48,009
In the thirteen twenties,
the Mexica people,

16
00:00:48,079 --> 00:00:50,699
the Aztecs, founded their
capital, Tenochtitlan,

17
00:00:50,739 --> 00:00:52,809
on a small island in the middle of a lake:

18
00:00:52,959 --> 00:00:57,289
Lake Texcoco, high in a valley
surrounded by mountains and volcanoes.

19
00:00:58,172 --> 00:01:01,192
They connected the island to
the shore with long causeways,

20
00:01:01,232 --> 00:01:05,432
built canals instead of streets, and grew
food on floating gardens made of mud and

21
00:01:05,472 --> 00:01:07,042
reeds, called chinampas.

22
00:01:07,492 --> 00:01:11,282
By the time the Spanish arrived, it was
one of the largest cities in the world.

23
00:01:12,124 --> 00:01:15,014
When Spanish soldiers first
saw it in fifteen nineteen,

24
00:01:15,084 --> 00:01:16,434
they couldn't believe their eyes.

25
00:01:16,884 --> 00:01:21,044
One of them, Bernal Díaz, later wrote that
the towers and temples rising out of the

26
00:01:21,084 --> 00:01:23,674
water looked like something
from a book of enchantments.

27
00:01:23,984 --> 00:01:26,974
Some of the soldiers, he said,
asked whether it was all a dream.

28
00:01:27,812 --> 00:01:31,672
In fifteen twenty-one, the Spanish
conquered Tenochtitlan and built their own

29
00:01:31,712 --> 00:01:33,782
capital right on top of its ruins.

30
00:01:34,232 --> 00:01:35,462
But they didn't like the lake.

31
00:01:36,295 --> 00:01:38,495
The valley has no natural
outlet to the sea,

32
00:01:38,535 --> 00:01:41,375
so in the rainy season, the
water had nowhere to go,

33
00:01:41,415 --> 00:01:43,685
and the new city flooded again and again.

34
00:01:44,155 --> 00:01:47,325
So, over the following centuries,
they decided to get rid of the lake.

35
00:01:47,535 --> 00:01:50,465
They dug canals and tunnels through
the mountains and drained it.

36
00:01:50,735 --> 00:01:52,225
It took a very long time.

37
00:01:52,495 --> 00:01:55,785
In sixteen twenty-nine, a huge
flood put the city under water,

38
00:01:55,935 --> 00:01:58,865
and parts of it stayed
flooded for about five years.

39
00:01:59,295 --> 00:02:03,335
People moved around in canoes, and the
engineer in charge of the drainage tunnel was

40
00:02:03,375 --> 00:02:04,385
thrown in jail.

41
00:02:05,331 --> 00:02:06,361
The lake disappeared.

42
00:02:06,531 --> 00:02:08,511
The city spread across the old lakebed.

43
00:02:08,551 --> 00:02:09,781
And that is the problem.

44
00:02:09,871 --> 00:02:12,521
Because what's left under
Mexico City isn't rock.

45
00:02:12,631 --> 00:02:13,901
It's the bottom of a lake.

46
00:02:16,259 --> 00:02:19,349
Under much of the city lies
a thick layer of soft clay,

47
00:02:19,439 --> 00:02:20,509
the old mud of the lake.

48
00:02:20,679 --> 00:02:24,109
And that clay is mostly water, like
a sponge that's soaked through.

49
00:02:24,952 --> 00:02:29,022
Below the clay are aquifers, layers of
rock and gravel full of groundwater.

50
00:02:29,232 --> 00:02:32,882
And for more than a century, Mexico
City has been drinking from them.

51
00:02:33,112 --> 00:02:36,832
Today, around sixty percent of the city's
water is pumped up from under its own

52
00:02:36,872 --> 00:02:37,362
streets.

53
00:02:38,330 --> 00:02:41,920
Pump the water out, and the clay
above slowly gives up its water too.

54
00:02:42,090 --> 00:02:45,140
It shrinks, the way a sponge
flattens when you squeeze it.

55
00:02:45,190 --> 00:02:47,120
And the city on top goes down with it.

56
00:02:47,924 --> 00:02:49,314
And here's the worst part.

57
00:02:49,524 --> 00:02:52,094
Once that clay is squeezed,
it doesn't spring back.

58
00:02:52,324 --> 00:02:55,754
Even if the pumping stopped tomorrow,
the ground would not come back up.

59
00:02:55,964 --> 00:02:57,114
The sinking is permanent.

60
00:02:58,087 --> 00:03:02,197
In the last century, parts of the city
have dropped by more than twelve meters.

61
00:03:02,507 --> 00:03:04,477
That's the height of a
four-storey building.

62
00:03:05,216 --> 00:03:10,196
In twenty twenty-six, a new radar satellite
built by NASA and India's space agency

63
00:03:10,236 --> 00:03:11,806
mapped the city from orbit.

64
00:03:12,176 --> 00:03:15,286
It found areas sinking by more
than two centimeters a month.

65
00:03:16,166 --> 00:03:19,776
You can see the damage from space, but
you can also see it from the street.

66
00:03:20,186 --> 00:03:21,056
Pavements tilt.

67
00:03:21,306 --> 00:03:23,116
Old buildings lean on their neighbors.

68
00:03:23,286 --> 00:03:27,106
And at the base of some monuments, the
ground has dropped so far that new steps had

69
00:03:27,146 --> 00:03:28,876
to be added, over and over again.

70
00:03:31,247 --> 00:03:33,507
When the ground sinks
evenly, you barely notice.

71
00:03:33,547 --> 00:03:36,587
The trouble starts when it sinks unevenly.

72
00:03:36,627 --> 00:03:39,177
One corner of a building
drops faster than the other,

73
00:03:39,227 --> 00:03:40,957
and the whole thing starts to lean.

74
00:03:41,874 --> 00:03:44,684
Look at the Metropolitan
Cathedral on the main square,

75
00:03:44,854 --> 00:03:45,664
the Zócalo.

76
00:03:46,214 --> 00:03:47,974
Construction began in
the fifteen hundreds,

77
00:03:48,014 --> 00:03:50,344
on top of the ruins of Aztec temples.

78
00:03:50,754 --> 00:03:53,864
For centuries, it has been
sinking unevenly into the clay.

79
00:03:54,755 --> 00:03:57,705
Right next to it lie the
ruins of the Templo Mayor,

80
00:03:57,875 --> 00:03:59,265
the great Aztec temple.

81
00:03:59,575 --> 00:04:01,515
They were rediscovered in
nineteen seventy-eight,

82
00:04:01,555 --> 00:04:05,705
when electricity workers digging under
the street hit a giant carved stone.

83
00:04:06,645 --> 00:04:09,255
By the nineteen nineties,
it was in serious trouble.

84
00:04:09,605 --> 00:04:13,555
One side of the foundation was more than
one and a half meters lower than the other,

85
00:04:13,605 --> 00:04:15,475
and cracks were running
through the building.

86
00:04:16,437 --> 00:04:18,547
So engineers did something surprising.

87
00:04:19,057 --> 00:04:22,697
Instead of pushing the low side up, they
carefully dug soil out from under the high

88
00:04:22,737 --> 00:04:26,307
side, to let it sink too, and bring
the cathedral back toward level.

89
00:04:26,637 --> 00:04:29,327
Then they injected grout into
the ground to slow it down.

90
00:04:30,212 --> 00:04:33,082
A few blocks away is
the Palace of Fine Arts,

91
00:04:33,172 --> 00:04:37,662
the Palacio de Bellas Artes, with its
white marble walls and golden domes.

92
00:04:37,772 --> 00:04:39,462
Heavy marble, on soft clay.

93
00:04:39,812 --> 00:04:41,822
Since construction began
in nineteen-oh-four,

94
00:04:42,112 --> 00:04:43,602
it has sunk by several meters.

95
00:04:44,616 --> 00:04:47,126
All over the city, you
can see the same story.

96
00:04:47,376 --> 00:04:48,286
Leaning churches.

97
00:04:48,676 --> 00:04:50,466
Buildings tilting toward each other.

98
00:04:50,676 --> 00:04:52,066
Streets that crack and buckle.

99
00:04:52,236 --> 00:04:54,326
Doors and windows that no longer close.

100
00:04:55,213 --> 00:04:58,023
The soft lake clay has
another dangerous side.

101
00:04:58,233 --> 00:05:00,413
When an earthquake hits,
it shakes like jelly,

102
00:05:00,453 --> 00:05:03,663
and it can make the shaking much
stronger than on solid rock.

103
00:05:04,546 --> 00:05:06,476
On the nineteenth of September,
nineteen eighty-five,

104
00:05:06,706 --> 00:05:08,916
a powerful earthquake
struck the Pacific coast,

105
00:05:09,026 --> 00:05:10,776
more than three hundred kilometers away.

106
00:05:11,126 --> 00:05:13,236
On solid ground, it did limited damage.

107
00:05:13,326 --> 00:05:15,896
But in the old lakebed, the
clay amplified the waves.

108
00:05:16,326 --> 00:05:17,456
Hundreds of buildings collapsed.

109
00:05:17,726 --> 00:05:19,666
The official death toll
was around ten thousand,

110
00:05:19,706 --> 00:05:21,636
and many believe the
real number was higher.

111
00:05:23,477 --> 00:05:25,107
And now the strangest part.

112
00:05:25,297 --> 00:05:27,687
A city built on a lake is
running short of water.

113
00:05:28,292 --> 00:05:30,932
The rest of the city's
water comes from far away,

114
00:05:30,972 --> 00:05:34,502
through the Cutzamala system:
reservoirs in the mountains,

115
00:05:34,672 --> 00:05:38,222
and water pumped uphill over a
mountain range to reach the capital.

116
00:05:39,116 --> 00:05:41,446
In twenty twenty-four,
after years of drought,

117
00:05:41,576 --> 00:05:44,666
those reservoirs fell to around
a quarter of their capacity.

118
00:05:45,156 --> 00:05:48,406
Headlines warned of a "Day Zero",
when the taps could run dry.

119
00:05:49,297 --> 00:05:50,137
It didn't happen.

120
00:05:50,177 --> 00:05:51,367
The rains came back.

121
00:05:51,417 --> 00:05:55,157
But for millions of people, water
already arrives only a few days a week,

122
00:05:55,197 --> 00:05:56,067
or by truck.

123
00:05:56,881 --> 00:06:00,721
Across the city, water trucks called
pipas drive from street to street,

124
00:06:00,761 --> 00:06:02,631
filling tanks on rooftops.

125
00:06:02,861 --> 00:06:05,071
On some streets, people
line up with buckets.

126
00:06:05,960 --> 00:06:08,190
And a lot of it never even reaches a tap.

127
00:06:08,480 --> 00:06:12,110
As the ground sinks, it bends and
breaks the pipes beneath the streets.

128
00:06:12,520 --> 00:06:16,410
By some estimates, around forty percent
of the city's water is lost to leaks.

129
00:06:17,361 --> 00:06:19,951
Then, in the rainy season,
the opposite happens.

130
00:06:20,281 --> 00:06:22,251
Heavy storms hit, and the streets flood.

131
00:06:22,581 --> 00:06:26,151
For five hundred years, the city's
water system was built to do one thing:

132
00:06:26,261 --> 00:06:28,651
get water out of the
valley as fast as possible.

133
00:06:29,593 --> 00:06:32,783
So Mexico City floods in
summer, runs dry in spring,

134
00:06:32,893 --> 00:06:34,123
and sinks all year round.

135
00:06:36,178 --> 00:06:37,548
Is there any way to stop the sinking?

136
00:06:38,107 --> 00:06:42,027
Scientists studying the clay say the
squeezing that has already started will keep

137
00:06:42,067 --> 00:06:43,557
going for a long time.

138
00:06:43,907 --> 00:06:47,547
One study estimated it could continue
for around a hundred and fifty years,

139
00:06:47,587 --> 00:06:50,537
and add up to thirty meters
more in the worst places.

140
00:06:51,515 --> 00:06:53,365
Pumping less would slow it down.

141
00:06:53,435 --> 00:06:55,545
So the city is trying to find other water:

142
00:06:55,615 --> 00:06:58,465
collecting rain from rooftops,
fixing leaking pipes,

143
00:06:58,595 --> 00:07:00,485
and pushing more water
back into the ground.

144
00:07:01,429 --> 00:07:05,239
And there's one place where you can
still see what this valley used to be.

145
00:07:05,289 --> 00:07:08,939
In Xochimilco, in the south of
the city, the old canals survive,

146
00:07:09,069 --> 00:07:10,909
with colorful boats and floating gardens,

147
00:07:10,949 --> 00:07:12,939
the last pieces of the great lake.

148
00:07:13,829 --> 00:07:19,109
Today, Xochimilco and the historic center
of Mexico City are a UNESCO World Heritage

149
00:07:19,149 --> 00:07:19,599
Site.

150
00:07:19,909 --> 00:07:22,269
On weekends, families
float along the canals,

151
00:07:22,309 --> 00:07:26,299
with music, food and flowers,
on water the Aztecs once farmed.

152
00:07:28,592 --> 00:07:30,102
So, why is Mexico City sinking?

153
00:07:30,567 --> 00:07:32,027
Because it was built on a lake.

154
00:07:32,067 --> 00:07:33,377
Because the lake was drained.

155
00:07:33,647 --> 00:07:37,557
And because the city still drinks the
water that holds up the ground beneath it.

156
00:07:38,274 --> 00:07:40,834
Venice is a city fighting
the water around it.

157
00:07:40,874 --> 00:07:41,024
Mexico City is a city that's
running out of the water under it.

158
00:07:44,983 --> 00:07:47,523
And the Angel of Independence
keeps standing still,

159
00:07:47,563 --> 00:07:50,973
while the city slowly drops
around it, one step at a time.

160
00:07:51,653 --> 00:07:54,583
So, would you live in a city
that sinks a little every day?

161
00:07:54,653 --> 00:07:56,583
And which city should we explain next?

162
00:07:56,713 --> 00:07:57,703
Tell me in the comments.

163
00:07:58,033 --> 00:07:59,143
This was How It's Run.
